  <h2>Korean Food — Bold, Fermented, Communal</h2>
  <p>Korean cuisine is one of the world's great food traditions. It's deeply communal — meals are shared, banchan (side dishes) cover the table, and eating alone is practically a sin. The flavors are bold: fermented, umami-rich, fiery, and deeply satisfying. A trip to Seoul without exploring the food scene is a trip half-lived.</p>

  <h2>🥩 Korean BBQ (삼겹살 / 갈비)</h2>
  <p>The quintessential Korean dining experience. You grill meat at your own table — samgyeopsal (pork belly) or galbi (marinated beef short ribs) — and wrap it in ssam (lettuce leaves) with garlic, sliced chili, and ssamjang (fermented paste). Accompanied by banchan: kimchi, bean sprouts, spinach, fish cake. Always with soju or beer.</p>
  <p><strong>Best areas:</strong> Mapo-gu (for samgyeopsal), Mapo Galmaegi (galbi), any neighborhood really</p>

  <h2>🍚 Bibimbap (비빔밥)</h2>
  <p>A bowl of steamed rice topped with seasoned vegetables (spinach, bean sprouts, mushrooms, carrot), a fried egg, and gochujang (red chili paste). Mix everything vigorously before eating. The dolsot (stone pot) version arrives sizzling — the rice crisps against the hot stone, creating a crunchy bottom layer called nurungji. Extraordinary.</p>

  <h2>🌶️ Tteokbokki (떡볶이)</h2>
  <p>Chewy cylindrical rice cakes simmered in a glossy, spicy-sweet gochujang sauce with fish cakes and boiled eggs. Korea's most beloved street food and comfort food. Found at pojangmacha tents, street stalls in Myeongdong, and dedicated tteokbokki restaurants. Beware — it's addictive.</p>

  <h2>🥘 Kimchi Jjigae (김치찌개)</h2>
  <p>A bubbling, deeply savory stew of fermented kimchi, pork belly, tofu, and vegetables. Best when the kimchi is well-aged (묵은지 — at least 6 months fermented). This is the dish Koreans eat when they're sick, tired, or homesick. It's pure comfort in a pot. Order it at any Korean restaurant; it's almost always on the menu.</p>

  <h2>🍗 Chimaek — Fried Chicken + Beer</h2>
  <p>Korea's greatest culinary gift to the world. Incredibly crispy fried chicken (not greasy — it's double-fried) paired with ice-cold beer (maekju). Best flavors: yangnyeom (sweet chili glaze), garlic butter, or soy sauce. Have it at a Han River picnic or at any chimaek restaurant. Order through Baemin app for delivery.</p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    💡 <strong>Korean chicken chains:</strong> BBQ Chicken, Kyochon, BHC, and Nene Chicken all have branches near tourist areas. Quality is consistently excellent.
  </div>

  <h2>🍨 Bingsu (빙수)</h2>
  <p>Korean shaved ice dessert. The traditional patbingsu has red bean paste, rice cakes, and condensed milk over finely shaved ice. Modern versions come with mango, strawberry, matcha, or Oreo. The portions are enormous — usually shared between two. Look for dedicated bingsu cafes in Insadong and Hongdae.</p>

  <h2>🥟 Mandu (만두) — Korean Dumplings</h2>
  <p>Korean dumplings filled with pork, kimchi, or vegetables. Steamed (찐만두), pan-fried (군만두), or in soup (만두국). Gwangjang Market has legendary handmade mandu stalls that have operated for generations.</p>

  <h2>Best Food Streets & Markets</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Gwangjang Market (광장시장)</strong> — Korea's oldest market. Bindaetteok (mung bean pancakes), mayak gimbap (mini seaweed rolls), raw beef (yukhoe). A must-visit.</li>
    <li><strong>Myeongdong Street Food</strong> — Tteokbokki, tornado potato, egg bread. Evening only.</li>
    <li><strong>Tongin Market</strong> — Traditional Korean side dishes using a token-based system. Unique experience.</li>
    <li><strong>Noryangjin Fish Market</strong> — Live seafood, buy it fresh, take it upstairs to be prepared. Incredible sashimi.</li>
    <li><strong>Mangwon Market</strong> — Local neighborhood market. Less touristy, more authentic.</li>
  </ul>

